esp_lvgl_port: graceful I2C error handling in touchpad read

The GT911 capacitive touch controller communicates over I2C.  Transient
bus errors (noise, contention with other I2C devices) cause
esp_lcd_touch_read_data() to return ESP_ERR_INVALID_RESPONSE.  The stock
esp_lvgl_port wraps both read_data and get_data in ESP_ERROR_CHECK, which
calls abort() on any non-OK return — a single I2C glitch kills the
firmware.

Fix: replace ESP_ERROR_CHECK with an early return that reports
LV_INDEV_STATE_RELEASED (no touch).  The LVGL timer will retry on the
next cycle (~30 ms later), so the touch recovers silently.

--- a/src/lvgl9/esp_lvgl_port_touch.c
+++ b/src/lvgl9/esp_lvgl_port_touch.c
@@ -124,10 +124,16 @@

     /* Read data from touch controller into memory */
-    ESP_ERROR_CHECK(esp_lcd_touch_read_data(touch_ctx->handle));
+    if (esp_lcd_touch_read_data(touch_ctx->handle) != ESP_OK) {
+        data->state = LV_INDEV_STATE_RELEASED;
+        return;
+    }

     /* Read data from touch controller */
-    ESP_ERROR_CHECK(esp_lcd_touch_get_data(touch_ctx->handle, touch_data, &touch_cnt, CONFIG_ESP_LCD_TOUCH_MAX_POINTS));
+    if (esp_lcd_touch_get_data(touch_ctx->handle, touch_data, &touch_cnt, CONFIG_ESP_LCD_TOUCH_MAX_POINTS) != ESP_OK) {
+        data->state = LV_INDEV_STATE_RELEASED;
+        return;
+    }
